; Order-independence of the weighted count of a disjunction of two
; rescalings of the same test. Pointwise, (z = 5 OR 2z = 10) is the
; wider slab, so both branches of the `if` carry the same evidence
; weight and P(c) = 1/2 — for BOTH operand orders of the `or`.
;
; The second query is the regression: with the coin for 2z = 10 first,
; the accepting BDD path {¬(2z=10), z=5} multiplies the z = 5 slab by
; the co-weight of 2z = 10 — the punctured slab. An implementation that
; collapses that product to zero undercounts the disjunction by half
; and answers P(c) = 1/3 here, so the `or` fails to commute.
(query
  (let (z (gaussian 0.0 1.0))
    (let (c (flip 0.5))
      (Posterior c (if c (or (real_eq z 5.0) (real_eq (*. 2.0 z) 10.0))
                         (real_eq z 5.0))))))
(query
  (let (z (gaussian 0.0 1.0))
    (let (c (flip 0.5))
      (Posterior c (if c (or (real_eq (*. 2.0 z) 10.0) (real_eq z 5.0))
                         (real_eq z 5.0))))))
